      "title": "File:Lancaster I NG128 Dropping Load - Duisburg - Oct 14 - 1944.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/Lancaster_I_NG128_Dropping_Load_-_Duisburg_-_Oct_14_-_1944.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lancaster_I_NG128_Dropping_Load_-_Duisburg_-_Oct_14_-_1944.jpg",
      "width": 1218,
      "height": 550,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "F/O L. Howard, No. 1 RAFFPU",
      "credit": "This photograph CL 1404 comes from the collections of the Imperial War Museums.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis photograph CL 1405 comes from the collections of the Imperial War Museums.",
      "source": "Lancaster I NG128 Dropping Load - Duisburg - Oct 14 - 1944",
      "description": "A Royal Air Force Avro Lancaster I (s/n NG128, coded \"SR-B\") of No. 101 Sqn out of Ludford Magna and flown by Fg Off R.B. Tibbs as part of a thousand-bomber raid, dropping its load over Duisburg, Germany, on 14-15 October 1944. Note the large aerials on top of the Lancaster´s fuselage, indicating that the aricraft is carrying 'Airborne Cigar' (ABC), a jamming device which disrupted enemy radio telephone channels. Over 2,000 sorties were dispatched to the city of Duisburg during 14-15 October 1944, in order to to demonstrate the RAF Bomber Command's overwhelming superiority in German skies (\"Operation Hurricane\"). \nLeft image:  the Lancaster releases bundles of 'Window' over the target during a special daylight raid on Duisburg.\nRight image:  a fraction of a second later, the aircraft releases the main part of its load, a 4000lb HC \"cookie\" and 108 30lb \"J\" incendiaries.",
      "dateCreated": "between 14 October 1944 and 15 October 1944<div style=\"display: none;\">date QS:P,+1944-10-00T00:00:00Z/10,P1319,+1944-10-14T00:00:00Z/11,P1326,+1944-10-15T00:00:00Z/11</div>",
